WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.370
There are some details that go by so quickly
in the final trailer for Todd Phillips' Joker

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:08.730
you could hardly be blamed for not noticing
them, while others are subtle thematic touches

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.440
that speak to exactly what kind of Joker we're
dealing with here.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:15.600
With that in mind, here's a look at some of
the small details you may have missed in the

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:16.320
final Joker trailer

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.440
While Arthur's mother Penny is prominently
featured in the first Joker trailer, we don't

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:22.720
see her much this time around.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.840
She's only in a couple of brief shots and
her face is covered when she appears.

00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:30.300
But what's happening to Penny in the final
trailer may be the main catalyst that sparks

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.580
Fleck's transformation — it appears as if
Arthur's mother is dying.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:38.200
When Arthur realizes talk show host Murray
Franklin is on TV and talking about him, he's

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.320
in his mother's hospital room.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:44.290
We briefly see Penny in her bed on the right,
with a breathing apparatus attached to her

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:46.160
face, possibly with a breathing tube inserted.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.040
If that's the case, then there's a good chance
Penny Fleck isn't long for this world.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.160
Her death, if it happens, could be enough
to finally push Arthur over the edge.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:04.300
We hear Joaquin Phoenix's chilling Joker cackle
early in the final trailer, though the circumstances

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:05.540
surrounding it are strange.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:09.400
Fleck is walking down a hallway in an unknown
building while buttoning up a shirt.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.060
Considering the creepy clown stuff in the
background and the dressing room lights similar

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:16.920
to the ones seen in the first trailer, this
is likely the headquarters for his professional

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.540
clowning gig, and he's just ended his shift.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.360
It's interesting because it's a very Joker
laugh, but it's belted out in a very non-Joker

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:24.860
way.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.600
It seems like maybe Fleck is laughing just
to placate his coworkers, and that's why he

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.800
stops as soon as he's out of earshot.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:34.420
It suggests the Joker is something of a persona
Fleck is slowly building.

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:37.820
It began as a front — something to protect
himself from social situations he's not equipped

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:38.320
to handle.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.120
But what is initially a ruse — no more real
than the makeup or the clown costume he wears

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.420
— will soon become his "true" self.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:46.120
The Joker likes kids.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.400
Or, at least, Arthur Fleck does.

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:52.280
There's a brief shot toward the end of the
final trailer of Fleck clowning around for

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:56.229
kids in a hospital, and early in the trailer
he's making faces to entertain a boy on a

00:01:56.229 --> 00:01:57.140
Gotham City bus.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.270
We also see him trying to entertain the young
Bruce Wayne in the first Joker trailer, though

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:05.789
it's possible he has an ulterior motive for
that, since Bruce's dad, Thomas Wayne, is

00:02:05.789 --> 00:02:07.040
clearly his adversary.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:11.440
Typically, if the Joker has anything to do
with children at all, he's being his typical

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.220
menacing self.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:16.830
A Joker who seems to genuinely enjoy entertaining
kids is intriguing — though most of the

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:20.840
interactions we've seen this Joker have with
children seem to be before his transformation

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.340
into the Clown Prince of Crime.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.180
It will be interesting to see if his love
for children survives.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.580
In the first Joker trailer, we only see Thomas
Wayne on television.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:33.829
This time, we see Arthur Fleck and Wayne standing
face to face, and the confrontation gets heated.

00:02:33.829 --> 00:02:36.450
"Is this a joke to you?"

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:40.090
We don't know what Thomas is talking about,
but it's possible the timing of of the Wayne/Fleck

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:42.659
confrontation in the trailer may contain a
clue.

00:02:42.659 --> 00:02:46.420
Right before we see Wayne throw his punch,
we get two shots that were also in the first

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.980
trailer — Fleck smashing his head against
a metal mesh barrier while grabbing for a

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.780
file and then running down a hallway, looking
as if he's being chased, still holding the

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:54.280
file.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.120
What file does Fleck steal?

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:00.340
It looks like he's in Arkham State Hospital,
and he's wearing the same outfit he wore while

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:02.800
walking across the street to the hospital
in the first trailer.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:04.469
Is it a patient file?

00:03:04.469 --> 00:03:06.700
Belonging to someone close to Thomas Wayne,
perhaps?

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:11.099
We've heard a lot about comedian Marc Maron
getting cast in Joker.

00:03:11.099 --> 00:03:14.940
The news raised a few eyebrows particularly
because Maron has been outspoken about his

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.910
low opinion of superhero movies.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:20.670
The new trailer gives us our first look at
Maron as Ted Marco, and it's quick.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:24.370
He appears toward the end with Murray and
Fleck, as the latter asks to be introduced

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:24.860
as "Joker."

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:29.780
For a while it wasn't completely clear exactly
what Maron's role in Joker would be, but in

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:34.810
mid-June 2019 while promoting Sword of Trust,
he opened up about Marco, saying,

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.709
"I basically play De Niro's producer in a
quick scene."

00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:41.270
He added that his role isn't very big, but
that the scene itself is pivotal.

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:45.670
Maron went on to say that his character "is
a guy trying to manage the whole situation"

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:47.390
between Fleck and Murray.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:51.280
This could mean that Fleck hasn't forgiven
Murray for humiliating him, as we see earlier

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.370
in the trailer, and that Ted Marco's there
as a peacemaker.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:57.769
But considering that Murray made a fool out
of the clearly unhinged Fleck while he was

00:03:57.769 --> 00:04:02.209
struggling with his mother's illness, Marco
likely won't succeed in his little diplomatic

00:04:02.209 --> 00:04:02.760
endeavor.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:07.170
Toward the end of the trailer we see a melee
on a subway between the police and a bunch

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:08.280
of Joker's followers.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.080
In the middle of it, Joker — even though
he's already in full clown makeup — puts

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:15.680
on one of his followers' clown masks, possibly
to disguise himself.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.740
If you thought this looked familiar, well,
it totally is.

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:22.840
This sequence is reminiscent of Christopher
Nolan's The Dark Knight, and Heath Ledger's

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:23.900
legendary Joker.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:28.300
The first time Ledger's Joker is onscreen,
his back is to us while he holds the clown

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.540
mask he's about to put on as part of the bank
robbery.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:34.430
At the end of the opening scene, we learn
that even though he's been wearing a clown

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:38.780
mask, he's also been in his clown makeup the
entire time, just like Arthur Fleck.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:47.180
"I believe whatever doesn't kill you simply
makes you … stranger."

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:51.500
One of the most interesting things about Joker's
final trailer — and the first one — is

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:52.020
what we don't see.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:56.900
Even though we know to expect a bloody, violent
movie, we've seen almost no violence at all

00:04:56.919 --> 00:04:57.919
in the trailers.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.060
At least, no violence from Joker himself.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:04.780
We've seen him get punched by Thomas Wayne,
whacked with his own sign, and beaten up by

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:05.300
jerks on the subway.

00:05:05.319 --> 00:05:07.970
We've seen his followers clash with the cops.

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:09.940
But we have yet to see Joker himself attack
anyone.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:14.400
The only possible exception is a shot toward
the end of the final trailer when Fleck appears

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:18.520
to be stomping on something in an alley, but
it isn't clear if he's attacking a person

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:21.400
or just stomping out of frustration — either
seems likely.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.580
And yet one of the most memorable images from
the trailer looks like it comes right after

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:28.540
Joker committed homicide, as the newspaper
headline suggests.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.280
We see him in a bathroom, cleaning himself
off.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:35.980
At one point he dances slowly and we see what
appears to be smeared blood on his chin.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:40.940
For a character historically defined by violence,
we've seen almost none of it in these trailers.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:45.340
Saving Fleck's rage for the big screen is
a savvy move — and we'll know the full extent

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:47.540
of it when Joker hits theaters on October
4th.

